('P', '**Entitlement blocks forgiveness.** Across six studies, narcissistic entitlement predicted less forgiveness, more insistence on repayment, and slower growth in forgiveness over time, even after accounting for personality, offense severity, apology, closeness, and religiosity (Exline et al., 2004).'),
('P', '**Narcissism and faith.** In three studies of mostly Roman Catholic adults in Poland, narcissistic traits were linked to using religion for social or emotional gain, to seeing God as punishing and vengeful (for the more antagonistic forms), and to a belief that God **owes them** special favors. Narcissism was not clearly linked to being more or less religious overall (Tokarz et al., 2026).'),
('P', '**Humility can be taught.** In a randomized trial of 59 adults, a 7.5-hour humility workbook increased humility, forgivingness, and patience and reduced general negativity compared with a control group, for religious and non-religious participants alike (Lavelock et al., 2014). Participants did not have NPD.'),
('P', '**Broader evidence.** Across 97 outcome studies, therapy adapted to clients’ religious beliefs worked about as well as standard therapy for distress and added spiritual benefits (Captari et al., 2018). We found **no trial of Christian-integrated treatment for NPD** (literature search, November 1, 2026).'),
('P', '**Honest summary for teaching:**'),
